"""Migrate: bring files and settings over from another portlin.

This window runs as the user and touches no drive itself. Every action is
portlin-migrate, started as a child process through pkexec, and this is a
reader of what it prints: a JSON list of candidates, a JSON inventory, and
the ::stages / ::step / ::progress / ::bytes / ::files / ::warn / ::result
protocol while it copies. So the program that can be asked to read another
drive and write into this one is one command with a fixed set of verbs, and
the same verbs work from a terminal.

Three pages: where from, what to bring, and the copy. The copy page is
worked out by RunTracker from the protocol alone, so everything it shows
(every phase ahead, bytes, speed, time left) is what the tool reported, not
a guess the window made up.

A copy can be paused: the window writes "pause" down the tool's stdin, the
tool stops between files and unmounts the old drive. The plan file stays,
so the copy can be resumed, now or the next time the window opens; rsync
skips what is already here.
"""

